Here's a program that does this. It reads the string and the pattern from the user, compiles the pattern, and then counts the matching lines.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;

public class LineMatcher {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter the text: ");
        String text = scanner.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter the regular expression: ");
        String regex = scanner.nextLine();

        try {
            Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(regex);
            int count = countMatchingLines(text, pattern);
            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("Text:");
            System.out.println(text);
            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("Regular expression: " + regex);
            System.out.println("Number of matching lines: " + count);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: the regular expression is invalid.");
            System.out.println("Details: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        scanner.close();
    }

    private static int countMatchingLines(String text, Pattern pattern) {
        if (text == null || text.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int count = 0;
        String[] lines = text.split("\n");

        for (String line : lines) {
            Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(line);
            if (matcher.find()) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

The program splits the input on newline characters and checks each line separately, so it handles multiline text. It prints the text and the pattern back to you, so you can see exactly what was matched against.

The `split("\n")` call treats a blank line as an empty string, which is fine for most purposes. If you'd rather count lines that are completely empty, change the loop to `if (line.isEmpty())`.

One thing to know is that `split` drops trailing empty strings. A text that ends in a newline, like `"a\nb\n"`, gives you two lines, `"a"` and `"b"`, and the final newline counts as nothing. If you need that last line counted, use `Pattern.splitAsStream(pattern, text)` or read the lines yourself with a `BufferedReader`.

There's also a case where a pattern can match across a newline. With the pattern `(a\nb)` and the text `"a\nb\nc"`, the program counts one line, because the pattern never sees the newline, and `split` has already cut the text. If you want that kind of match to count, don't split. Read the whole text as one block and count each match, and remember to divide by the number of lines each match covers, since a match that spans two lines should count for both.

